WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:05.150
We have seen both while loops and for loops
in our coverage of loops.

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:10.760
While loops are used for conditional situations,
and for loops are run a set number of times.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:16.940
You may sometimes want one loop inside another
when you have multiple loops to run.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:21.580
Having one loop inside of another is known
as having a nested loop.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:27.420
Here is a pseudo-gaming situation: a player
has three lives with a goal to get into 10

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.770
positions in each life.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:33.660
The player can make these position goals as
long as the game progresses.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.480
We want updates each time a player makes a
position.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.730
To accomplish this goal, look at the following
code in the 226-nested file.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:50.030
On line 1, we have a Boolean variable, game_state,
set to True when the game is being played.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:55.890
On line 2, we have a game_lives variable set
to 1.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:02.500
Lines 3 through 7 have a while statement with
both a loop and a decision flow.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:09.180
The while statement is necessary because we
have a condition: run this code as long as

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.820
game_lives is less than 3.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:18.270
The for loop runs through, 10 times, an indicator
of what positions the player reached and during

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.020
which life those positions were reached.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.869
Following the for loop, a player moves on
to the next life so long as the game is still

00:01:25.869 --> 00:01:30.110
playing, as indicated on lines 6 and 7.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:35.780
This code could have many more logical situations,
like a game being paused, but this topic's

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:40.149
focus is nested loops, which is why I left
that logic out.

00:01:40.149 --> 00:01:46.130
Finally, line 8 shows a thank you statement
as a print statement.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.540
I will run this code.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.479
The code works, but we could use two adjustments.

00:01:52.479 --> 00:02:00.890
First, the positions should run 1 through
10, not 0 through 9.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:06.350
Secondly, the code stops at the second life.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:11.290
What two adjustments do you see to be made
to the code?

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:17.060
The first adjustment should be on line 3,
where we need to change the operator to less

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:24.349
than or equal so that the code loops through
this while statement on the third life.

00:02:24.349 --> 00:02:31.610
Secondly, we need the range to go from 1 to
11, as, if you recall, a range with two numbers

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:35.879
starts on the first number and ends just before
the last number, this time just using the

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:41.240
numbers needed rather than a plus one, as
we did in a previous topic.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:51.771
I will make that change to the range on line
4, rerun the code, and we see the 10 position

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:56.040
messages inside each life.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.360
Knowing how to use nested loops allows one
to know when to run one process inside of

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.439
another, which is a common occurrence in programming
logic.

